  reusable layer, and [38;5;153mfile://[39m is a weak constraint if you're fine with [38;5;153mnpx serve[39m. I'd leave [38;5;153msrc/[39m alone — converting it
  is unrelated refactoring.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should the validation module be loaded by the page? → ES modules, web code only (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m [1mThe fork:[0m how much of the form plumbing the reusable layer owns. Today [38;5;153mapp.js[39m reads each input by id by hand, and
  reports failure with [38;5;153mconsole.error[39m — no user-visible error at all. Pure validation leaves that boilerplate to be
  re-written per form; a DOM binder absorbs it but couples the layer to markup conventions.

  - [1mPure core only[0m ([38;5;153mvalidate(values, schema) → { valid, errors }[39m) — buys a tiny, fully testable unit with no DOM
    dependency. Costs: form #2 still hand-writes value collection and error rendering. Cheap to add a binder on top
    later; nothing gets redone.
  - [1mCore + DOM binder[0m ([38;5;153mbindForm(formEl, schema, onValid)[39m reads fields, renders messages next to inputs) — buys genuinely
    cheap form #2: a schema and one call. Costs a markup convention (where error text goes) invented with no second
    form to validate it against, and it makes the module need a DOM to test.
  - [1mCore + binder + live validation[0m (also validate on blur/input) — buys better UX. Costs the most speculative surface
    of the three; I'd hold this back.

  [1mMy recommendation: pure core plus a thin binder, skipping live validation.[0m The binder is where the actual duplication
  lives, so core-only would under-deliver on "reusable." But I'd keep the binder deliberately dumb — find fields by
  [38;5;153mname[39m, put messages in a [38;5;153m<span data-error-for>[39m — so replacing it costs nothing if form #2 disagrees. Since the binder
  is the speculative half, core-only is a defensible call if you'd rather wait for a real second consumer.
